<?xml version="1.0"?>
<sdf version="1.9">
  <model name="lunar_terrain">
    <static>true</static>
    <!-- THE NUMBERS IN THIS FILE ARE GENERATED. Do not hand-tune them.
         selene_sim/scripts/generate_heightmap.py writes
         heightmaps/terrain_datum.json alongside the PNGs, and
         selene_sim/test/test_heightmap_datum.py FAILS if the three literals
         below (link pose z, and the two <size> z values) drift from it. That
         test needs no Gazebo and runs on every push; scripts/check_terrain.sh
         is the physical check.

         WHY A PIXEL IS NOT A FIXED NUMBER OF METRES HERE. gz-sim maps the
         maximum pixel value PRESENT IN THE IMAGE to <size> z - not the format
         maximum - and does not subtract the image minimum:
             z_render = size_z * pixel / max(pixel in image)
         MEASURED 2026-07-30 on gz-sim 8.14.0 with three synthetic heightmaps at
         <size>100 100 30</size>: a uniform 128 read 30.0000 (a format-max rule
         predicts 15.0588); 102 flat with 204 in one corner read 15.0000 (12.0000);
         153 flat with min 51 and max 255 read 18.0000, ruling out a (max-min)
         rule which predicts 15.0000. Exact to 4 dp in all three.

         So each image declares the metric height of ITS OWN brightest pixel, the
         generator guarantees that pixel is exactly the format maximum, and
         z_render then equals the metric height. The link pose carries
         -BASE_ELEVATION, so:
             world z = (metric height) - 15.0
         The datum is now the +15 m base the generator actually adds, not a fudge.

         WHAT THIS REPLACED. The generator used to emit (H/30)*65535 on a field
         whose true maximum is 24.2528 m, so the brightest pixel was 0.8084 of
         full range and gz-sim stretched it back out over 30 m - inflating every
         elevation by 1.2370 (visual) and 1.2500 (collision, whose decimated
         maximum landed on 204/255 = 0.8000). The old -18 m datum was compensating
         for that inflation, which is why it sat ~3 m from the +15 m base. The two
         images disagreeing also put the collision surface up to 0.24 m ABOVE the
         visual one, so robots drove on an invisible floor; that residual is now
         within +/-0.06 m, the 8-bit quantisation of the coarse map.

         The two <size> z values DIFFER, and must. Decimating the field to
         129x129 does not preserve its maximum (24.252795 -> 24.053652). Sharing
         one value would reintroduce the bug: normalising the coarse map against
         the fine maximum leaves its brightest pixel at 253, and gz-sim would
         renormalise by 253 and put the collision surface 0.199 m too high.

         Keep the offset on the LINK pose, not on <heightmap><pos>. MEASURED
         (Gazebo Harmonic 8.14.0): with <heightmap><pos>0 0 -18</pos> the plain
         STILL rendered ~18 m high, i.e. gz-sim ignores the heightmap's own pos
         offset. Moving the same value onto the link pose is what actually shifts
         the surface. Do not move it back. -->
    <link name="terrain_link">
      <pose>0 0 -15 0 0 0</pose>
      <collision name="terrain_collision">
        <geometry>
          <heightmap>
            <uri>model://lunar_terrain/heightmaps/lunar_collision_129.png</uri>
            <!-- GENERATED: collision.size_z_m in heightmaps/terrain_datum.json.
                 The metric height of this image's brightest pixel (255/255).
                 Coarser 8-bit collision heightmap: gz-sim heightmap collision is
                 unreliable at 513x513; measured fall-throughs dropped 3 -> 1 at
                 129x129 (3.91 m cells, 0.094 m per 8-bit level). The visual below
                 keeps the full 513 16-bit image. -->
            <size>500 500 24.053652</size>
            <pos>0 0 0</pos>
          </heightmap>
        </geometry>
        <surface>
          <friction>
            <ode>
              <mu>0.7</mu>
              <mu2>0.5</mu2>
            </ode>
          </friction>
        </surface>
      </collision>
      <visual name="terrain_visual">
        <geometry>
          <heightmap>
            <uri>model://lunar_terrain/heightmaps/lunar_surface_513.png</uri>
            <!-- GENERATED: visual.size_z_m in heightmaps/terrain_datum.json.
                 The metric height of this image's brightest pixel (65535/65535).
                 This is NOT the same number as the collision <size> above, and
                 must not be made the same - see the note on terrain_link. -->
            <size>500 500 24.252795</size>
            <pos>0 0 0</pos>
          </heightmap>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.3 0.3 0.3 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.5 0.5 0.5 1</diffuse>
          <specular>0.05 0.05 0.05 1</specular>
        </material>
      </visual>
    </link>
  </model>
</sdf>
